m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glycine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network integrates diverse prebiotic carbon and nitrogen sources (CO2, HCN, CH4, NH3), with conversion into simple organics (formaldehyde, ammonia, hydrogen cyanide, acetonitrile, glycolonitrile, methylamine, etc.) via hydrothermal and surface processes. Reductive amination, hydrolysis, Strecker-type reactions, and radical chemistry contribute multiple plausible routes. Some pathways merge intermediates derived from HCN and formaldehyde, while others use Strecker synthesis with aldehydes and ammonia or hydrothermal catalysis. This results in 10 distinct, literature-supported and chemically plausible synthesis pathways, traversing hydrothermal, surface, and biochemical/prebiotic environments, converging on glycine formation.\n\nMOLECULES (16 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (Carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (Ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CH4 (Methane) [starting]\n  - mol_004: HCN (Hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: HCHO (Formaldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_006: NH2CH2OH (Ethanolamine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: NH2COOH (Glycine) [target]\n  - mol_008: CH3CN (Acetonitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_009: NH2CH2CN (Aminoacetonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_010: NH2CHO (Formamide) [intermediate